  <!--
    Path data of the adaptive launcher icon, in dp on its 108 dp canvas.
    The foreground and the monochrome layer both reference these strings,
    so each peak outline exists once and the layers cannot diverge. Keep
    every group of both layers free of transforms, so that these numbers
    are the drawn positions.

    Safe zone: a launcher may mask everything outside a circle of 66 dp
    diameter around (54, 54). Keep every control point of every outline
    within 33 dp of that center, and clip every other path to an outline.

    Outlines (ic_launcher_peak_*): each peak is a polygon of five vertices
    on a 0.25 dp grid. About x = 54, the right peak mirrors the left one,
    and the middle peak mirrors itself.
      left:   (27, 74) (27, 47.25) (38.5, 38) (43, 41.5) (43, 74)
      middle: (45.5, 76) (45.5, 36) (54, 28.25) (62.5, 36) (62.5, 76)
      right:  (65, 74) (65, 41.5) (69.5, 38) (81, 47.25) (81, 74)
    A circular arc rounds each vertex, with a radius of 6 dp at the bases
    and the shoulders and 3 dp at the apexes. For a vertex with turning
    angle phi and radius r, the arc meets each edge r * tan(phi / 2) from
    the vertex. It is one cubic whose handles lie along the edges and are
    4 / 3 * tan(phi / 4) * r long. The arcs on one edge do not overlap, so
    every edge keeps a straight part. Each outline starts on its left edge
    above the base and runs clockwise.

    Snow regions (ic_launcher_snow_*): each follows the freely drawn snow
    line of its peak, continues 1 dp past each column edge along the end
    tangent of the line, and closes with a box up to y = 26. Both layers
    clip each region to the outline of its peak, and the foreground clips
    the highlight band and the small highlight the same way, so the
    outline alone bounds them.

    Snow gap: the monochrome layer draws each peak as its snow region and
    a rock region below it, so the themed icon keeps the snow line of the
    color icon as a gap. The rock regions stay inline in that layer, their
    only user. The top of a rock region is the snow line offset 1.75 dp
    along its normal: move each leg of the control polygon of the line
    1.75 dp toward the rock, put each inner control point where its two
    moved legs meet, or move it with them where the legs are collinear,
    and move each end point with its leg. A vertical
    shift would narrow the gap on the steep parts of the line; this
    offset keeps it between 1.74 and 1.87 dp. Like the snow line, the top
    continues 1 dp past each column edge along its end tangent, and the
    region closes with a box down to y = 78, below every base. The gap is
    about 3 px wide at 48 dp on a 420 dpi screen, so it stays open at
    launcher size, and it is narrower than the 2.5 dp gaps between the
    columns, which stay the main division.

    Write path data with absolute M, L, C, and Z commands, one per line,
    with at most three decimals.
  -->
